Rodeo is open source. Different parts of this repository use different licences,
chosen so that the platform can be built into other products, open or closed, while
the Rodeo designs themselves stay open.

Copyright (c) 2026 DOT RODEO LLC

HARDWARE: CERN-OHL-W-2.0
  All hardware design files: KiCad schematics and PCBs, Gerbers, BOMs, placement
  files, and mechanical/panel files (hardware/, rev-a/, and instruments/*/kicad/).
  Full text: LICENSES/CERN-OHL-W-2.0.txt
  Source location: https://github.com/dotrodeo/rodeo
                   (also https://rodeooh.rodeo/source, which redirects there)

  In plain terms: anyone may build, modify and sell Rodeo hardware. If you modify
  a Rodeo design and distribute it, your modified design files must be published
  under the same licence. Products that use Rodeo boards, or connect to them
  through their published interfaces, do not have to be open.

SOFTWARE: MIT
  Firmware and the Rodeo SDK: rodeo-sdk/ (excluding its third-party submodules),
  instruments/rodeo-001/firmware/, instruments/rodeo-002/firmware/,
  rev-a/rodeo-encoder-hub/firmware/, tools/.
  Full text: LICENSES/MIT.txt

DOCUMENTATION: CC-BY-SA-4.0
  Documentation and written material: *.md files and docs/ folders.
  Full text: LICENSES/CC-BY-SA-4.0.txt

THIRD-PARTY COMPONENTS keep their own licences:
  rodeo-sdk/libDaisy  (MIT, Electrosmith)
  rodeo-sdk/DaisySP   (MIT, Electrosmith); its DaisySP-LGPL module is LGPL-2.1.
  Builds that enable DaisySP-LGPL (e.g. rodeo-002's ReverbSc) include LGPL code.
  Because the firmware source is published, those builds can be rebuilt and relinked
  as the LGPL requires; keep DaisySP-LGPL's licence notice with any binary you ship.

The name "Rodeo" and its logo are not covered by these licences. They may not be
used to brand a derivative product.
